// Change: update to existing rule
// Title: Trusted Path Bypass via Windows Directory Spoofing
// Sigma ID: 0cbe38c0-270c-41d9-ab79-6e5a9a669290
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Swachchhanda Shrawan Poudel (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2025-06-17 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: persistence, privilege-escalation, execution, stealth, T1574.007, T1548.002
// Description: Detects DLLs loading from a spoofed Windows directory path with an extra space (e.g "C:\Windows
//   \System32") which can bypass Windows trusted path verification. This technique tricks Windows
//   into treating the path as trusted, allowing malicious DLLs to load with high integrity
//   privileges bypassing UAC.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely
// References:
//   - https://x.com/Wietze/status/1933495426952421843
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ad147b5514596e70f2782769d9833046b7c8e12b/rules/windows/image_load/image_load_win_trusted_path_bypass.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceImageLoadEvents
| where FolderPath contains ":\\Windows \\System32\\" or FolderPath contains ":\\Windows \\SysWOW64\\"
